Silo patch 0017 — winecoreaudio: the device period is counted in the device's frames

Origin: camsteinberg/mavrik-engine `patches/0038-winecoreaudio-period-in-device-frames.patch` (Cam Steinberg,
Mavrik's own work, LGPL-2.1-or-later, at b018805, 2026-11-18). Code unchanged; it applies to our tree after 0007
(which carries the same upstream code Mavrik's 0009 does) with no fuzz. Mavrik offers it to WineHQ; upstream master
still has 0007's code unchanged (checked by Mavrik 2026-10-30).

Why: 0007 sets the Mac's I/O buffer (kAudioDevicePropertyBufferFrameSize) to the stream's period so the Mac pulls one
period at a time, but it counts the period in the stream's frames (nSamplesPerSec) and hands that number to the
device, whose buffer size and allowed range count frames at the device's nominal rate. A shared stream at another
rate than the device therefore got a pull longer or shorter than its period: a 48 kHz stream's 11.6 ms period (557
frames) became 557 device frames = 12.6 ms on a 44.1 kHz output. A pull longer than the refill is the starving
pattern 0007 exists to stop (Uncharted's crackle family). Now the period is converted to device frames at the
device's nominal rate (0007's get_device_sample_rate; the stream's rate if that read fails), clamped to the device's
range there and set; the stream's period is computed back from it, so a clamp still shows in the reported period.
At the same rate nothing changes.

Checked here: applies after 0002-0016 with no fuzz; coreaudio.c compiles with the runtime's flags with no warning.

diff --git a/dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c b/dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c
index 2d2fd8d..e99b54a 100644
--- a/dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c
+++ b/dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c
@@ -1264,7 +1264,7 @@ static HRESULT get_device_sample_rate(AudioDeviceID dev_id, EDataFlow flow,
 static NTSTATUS unix_create_stream(void *args)
 {
     struct create_stream_params *params = args;
-    unsigned int min_period_frames, max_period_frames;
+    unsigned int min_period_frames, max_period_frames, dev_period_frames, dev_rate;
     struct coreaudio_stream *stream;
     AURenderCallbackStruct input;
     OSStatus sc;
@@ -1300,9 +1300,18 @@ static NTSTATUS unix_create_stream(void *args)
                                                               &max_period_frames)))
         goto end;
 
-    stream->period_frames = max(min_period_frames, min(stream->period_frames, max_period_frames));
+    /* mavrik: the device's buffer size and its range count device frames, at the device's
+     * rate, while period_frames counts the stream's. Convert, so that a stream at another
+     * rate gets a Mac pull of one period, not of period_frames device frames (a 48 kHz
+     * stream on a 44.1 kHz device pulled 12.6 ms for an 11.6 ms period, more than the
+     * game refills). The same rate changes nothing. */
+    if (FAILED(get_device_sample_rate(stream->dev_id, stream->flow, &dev_rate)) || !dev_rate)
+        dev_rate = stream->fmt->nSamplesPerSec;
+    dev_period_frames = muldiv(stream->period_frames, dev_rate, stream->fmt->nSamplesPerSec);
+    dev_period_frames = max(min_period_frames, min(dev_period_frames, max_period_frames));
+    stream->period_frames = max(1, muldiv(dev_period_frames, stream->fmt->nSamplesPerSec, dev_rate));
     stream->period = muldiv(stream->period_frames, 10000000, stream->fmt->nSamplesPerSec);
-    if (FAILED(params->result = set_device_period_frame_size(stream->dev_id, stream->flow, stream->period_frames)))
+    if (FAILED(params->result = set_device_period_frame_size(stream->dev_id, stream->flow, dev_period_frames)))
         goto end;
 
     stream->bufsize_frames = muldiv(params->duration, stream->fmt->nSamplesPerSec, 10000000);
